            {
              "id": "ddc84ace-5ec8-31a5-80cb-abe32cd3b5ac",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding highlights a real risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to memcpy. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. There is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap, and the dataflow analysis suggests that the destination buffer may be used as the source as well. Additionally, the source pointer may be null at this point, which could also lead to a crash. The presence of a NUL assignment after the call does not mitigate the overlap risk. This is a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed to ensure memory safety.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(buf, s.data(), s.size());\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid potential crashes:\n\n```cpp\nif (s.data() != nullptr) {\n    memmove(buf, s.data(), s.size());\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
